6. ADMINISTRATOR RIGHTS

6.1 The Rusty Client launcher does not ask for administrator rights. It runs with the normal rights of your Windows account and starts Minecraft without administrator rights. Only the Rusty Client Setup program and the uninstaller ask Windows for administrator rights, because they install Rusty Client into the Program Files folder, create shortcuts for all users and register Rusty Client in Windows Apps. This also applies when Rusty Client updates itself (Section 10.5): the update is installed by the Rusty Client Setup program, so Windows asks for administrator rights at that moment.

6.2 You should only run official Rusty Client builds. Running a modified or unofficial build, especially with administrator rights, can seriously harm your computer, your files and your accounts. The Developer is not responsible for any version of Rusty Client that was not provided by the Developer.


7. MINECRAFT, MICROSOFT AND OTHER THIRD-PARTY SERVICES

7.1 Rusty Client is an independent project. Rusty Client and the Developer are not affiliated with, endorsed by, sponsored by or connected to Mojang, Microsoft, FabricMC, Modrinth or the authors of any Downloaded Mod in any way. "Minecraft" is a trademark of Mojang and Microsoft. It is used only to describe what Rusty Client works with. NOT AN OFFICIAL MINECRAFT PRODUCT. NOT APPROVED BY OR ASSOCIATED WITH MOJANG OR MICROSOFT.

7.2 Rusty Client does not contain Minecraft. When you play, the launcher downloads the official Minecraft game files, libraries, assets and Java runtime directly from the servers of Mojang, checks them with the checksums that Mojang publishes, places them in the Rusty Client folder on your computer and starts Minecraft. Minecraft, its content and all Minecraft services stay the property of Mojang and Microsoft and are subject only to their terms. You need your own Microsoft account that owns Minecraft: Java Edition.

7.3 Downloaded Mods. The launcher downloads Fabric Loader and Fabric API from FabricMC and Modrinth, and the performance mods Sodium, Lithium, FerriteCore, ImmediatelyFast and EntityCulling from Modrinth, directly onto your computer and keeps them up to date. Downloaded Mods are not part of Rusty Client and are not licensed to you under this Agreement. Each of them is licensed to you by its own authors under its own license. Mods that you add yourself are your own responsibility.

7.4 Your use of Minecraft is governed by the Minecraft End User License Agreement, the Minecraft Usage Guidelines, the Microsoft Services Agreement and all other rules of Mojang and Microsoft, which you accept directly with them, and by the rules of each server you join. You are solely responsible for following them. The Developer has no control over Minecraft or any server, cannot change your Microsoft account, cannot lift bans, cannot recover worlds or items, and gives no promise about what Mojang, Microsoft or any server owner does.

7.5 Fair play. Rusty Client is made for fair play. It does not include features that are meant to give an unfair advantage. Even so, some servers do not allow any modified client, or do not allow some features of Rusty Client (for example the HUD, zoom, freelook, waypoints or Toggle Sprint). It is your responsibility to check the rules of every server you join and to turn off any feature that is not allowed there. The Developer is not responsible if a server punishes or bans you.

7.6 Microsoft accounts. You sign in with your Microsoft account in a Microsoft sign-in window. Rusty Client never sees, asks for or stores your Microsoft password. After the sign-in, Rusty Client receives sign-in tokens from Microsoft, Xbox Live and the Minecraft services and uses them only to check that you own Minecraft and to start Minecraft with your account. You may only add accounts that you own or that you are clearly allowed to use, and you are responsible for everything that happens with them. Microsoft decides whether this sign-in keeps working and can end any sign-in at any time.

7.7 Rusty Client users. To show the Rusty Client logo next to the names of other Rusty Client users, the in-game client marks one otherwise unused bit in the skin settings that Minecraft sends to the server you play on. The server and every player who can see you can therefore see that you use Rusty Client. You can turn this off at any time in the in-game Settings page ("Show others that I use Rusty Client").

7.8 Mojang, Microsoft, FabricMC, Modrinth or a server owner can change or stop their services, files, download servers or rules at any time. This can stop Rusty Client from working, fully or partly, until Rusty Client is updated, or forever. The Developer is not responsible for this.

7.9 Rusty Client can open or use other third-party services, such as GitHub (to check for and download Rusty Client updates and to open the project page) and the official Chromium Embedded Framework download server. These services belong to their owners and have their own terms and privacy rules. The Developer is not responsible for them.


8. YOUR DATA AND PRIVACY

8.1 Rusty Client does not have its own user accounts, never asks for your Microsoft password, does not show advertising and does not contain tracking or telemetry. Rusty Client does not send any personal data to the Developer. Your Microsoft account tokens are only sent to Microsoft, Xbox Live and the Minecraft services.

8.2 Rusty Client connects to the internet only for its own functions. In version 1.0.4 these are: the Microsoft, Xbox Live and Minecraft sign-in services (login.live.com, user.auth.xboxlive.com, xsts.auth.xboxlive.com and api.minecraftservices.com); the Mojang version, game file, library, asset, Java runtime and news services (piston-meta.mojang.com, piston-data.mojang.com, launchermeta.mojang.com, libraries.minecraft.net, resources.download.minecraft.net and launchercontent.mojang.com) and www.minecraft.net for the version pictures; the FabricMC services (meta.fabricmc.net and maven.fabricmc.net); Modrinth (api.modrinth.com and cdn.modrinth.com) for Fabric API and the performance mods; the official Chromium Embedded Framework download server (cef-builds.spotifycdn.com), once, for the browser runtime that draws the launcher; and, for automatic updates, the GitHub release service and download servers (api.github.com, github.com and the GitHub file servers that github.com sends the download to, such as *.githubusercontent.com). Minecraft itself also connects to the servers you join and to the services of Mojang and Microsoft. Like any internet connection, these services can see your IP address and that a request was made. Their own privacy rules apply.

8.3 Rusty Client stores its files only on your computer, mainly in the folder %APPDATA%\.rustyclient. These are your launcher settings (settings.json), your accounts (accounts.dat), the Minecraft game files, libraries, assets and Java runtime, the downloaded browser runtime, the in-game client, one game folder per Minecraft version with its worlds, resource packs, screenshots, mods, configuration and logs, and your in-game client profiles. accounts.dat contains the name, id, skin information and sign-in tokens of every added account. It is encrypted with Windows (DPAPI) so that only your Windows user account on this computer can read it. When Minecraft starts, the launcher writes a launch log (rustyclient-launch.log) into the logs folder of that version. The access token is replaced by <hidden> in that log.

8.4 When the installed launcher downloads an update, it saves the new Rusty Client Setup program in your temporary folder (%TEMP%) and removes it again later. Crash reports and logs that Minecraft writes stay on your computer. They are never sent automatically. You decide whether you want to send them to the Developer, and if you do, you agree that the Developer may use them to find and fix problems.

8.5 The Profile page of the in-game client shows your name, UUID, skin, session time and similar information. It is only shown to you, and the UUID is hidden until you click it. Rusty Client does not send it anywhere.

8.6 You can remove all data that Rusty Client created by uninstalling Rusty Client and then deleting the folder %APPDATA%\.rustyclient yourself. Uninstalling keeps this folder on purpose, so your worlds are not lost.

8.7 If a later version of Rusty Client needs to process more or other data, the Developer will describe this in that version and, where the law requires it, ask for your consent first.


9. ACCEPTABLE USE OF THE IN-GAME CLIENT

9.1 The in-game client only changes what Minecraft shows on your own screen and how you control it. Everything it draws is only visible to you, except the Rusty Client marker described in Section 7.7.

9.2 You must not use Name Protect, screenshots, waypoints, profiles or any other feature of Rusty Client to deceive, harass, impersonate or harm other players, or to break the rules of a server.


10. UPDATES AND CHANGES

10.1 The Developer may, but does not have to, provide updates, fixes or new versions of Rusty Client. The Developer may add, change or remove features at any time, including features that you liked or used.

10.2 Each update or new version of Rusty Client is part of the Software and is covered by this Agreement, unless it comes with its own agreement, which then applies instead.

10.3 The Developer may change this Agreement for future versions of Rusty Client. The version of this Agreement that comes with a version of Rusty Client applies to that version. If you do not accept a changed Agreement, do not install or use the version that comes with it.

10.4 Rusty Client may stop working with older versions of Windows, with older versions of Rusty Client, with older or newer versions of Minecraft, or with changes made by Mojang, Microsoft, FabricMC or Modrinth. The Developer does not promise that any version of Rusty Client will keep working for any period of time.

10.5 Automatic updates. When the installed launcher starts, it checks the official Rusty Client releases on GitHub (https://github.com/elistrrdev/RustyClient/releases) for a newer version. If there is one, it downloads the Rusty Client Setup program of that version, checks it, closes itself and starts the Setup program, which installs the update into the folder where Rusty Client is already installed and then starts Rusty Client again. This happens automatically and Rusty Client does not ask you first, except for the administrator prompt of Windows. Automatic updates can not be turned off. If you do not want them, do not use Rusty Client. Only an installed copy of Rusty Client updates itself, test builds never do. Updates are not checked while Minecraft is running. By keeping Rusty Client installed and using it after an update, you accept the version of this Agreement that comes with that update. The current version of this Agreement is always available at https://github.com/elistrrdev/RustyClient/blob/main/LICENSE.


